Cthulhu: Ctrl/Cmd+W on a pinned tab UNLOADS it instead of doing nothing.

Upstream already refuses to close a pinned tab with the keyboard and
just moves the selection to the first unpinned tab, which makes the
shortcut feel inert. Pinned tabs here live beside the Home button as
long-lived fixtures, so the useful meaning of "close" on one is "drop
what it holds in memory but keep the tab" -- discardBrowser().

Upstream's own selection move is load-bearing: _mayDiscardBrowser()
refuses to discard the selected tab, so moving off it is what makes the
discard possible. With no unpinned tab to move to, the selection cannot
move and this is a no-op.

The Home tab is exempt. It is the one pinned tab that must stay
available and it is hidden from the strip, so unloading it would only
buy an invisible tab that reloads on the next Home press.

Not force-discarded: forcing skips beforeunload and dismisses open
dialogs, so a pinned tab holding an unsubmitted form would lose it
silently -- worse than the close this shortcut usually performs, which
at least prompts.

The discard waits for TabSwitchDone. Tearing the browser down while the
async switcher is still leaving it lands in onLayersCleared() with the
tab in an unexpected state and trips AsyncTabSwitcher's assertion: a
dump() in release builds, a throw in DEBUG ones.

diff --git a/browser/base/content/browser-commands.js b/browser/base/content/browser-commands.js
--- a/browser/base/content/browser-commands.js
+++ b/browser/base/content/browser-commands.js
@@ -370,14 +370,63 @@ var BrowserCommands = {
 
     // Keyboard shortcuts that would close a tab that is pinned select the first
     // unpinned tab instead.
+    //
+    // Cthulhu: and then UNLOAD that pinned tab. Upstream already refuses to
+    // close a pinned tab this way and merely moves the selection off it, which
+    // leaves the shortcut feeling like it did nothing. Pinned tabs here sit
+    // beside the Home button as long-lived fixtures, so the useful meaning of
+    // "close" on one is "drop what it is holding in memory but keep it" --
+    // which is exactly discardBrowser().
+    //
+    // Moving the selection first is not incidental: _mayDiscardBrowser()
+    // refuses to discard the selected tab, so upstream's own step is what
+    // makes the discard possible. If there is no unpinned tab to move to, the
+    // selection cannot move, the discard is refused, and this is a no-op.
+    //
+    // NOT force-discarded. Forcing skips the beforeunload check and dismisses
+    // open dialogs, so a pinned tab holding an unsubmitted form would lose it
+    // silently -- worse than the close this shortcut usually performs, which
+    // at least prompts. A tab that declines to unload simply stays loaded.
     if (
       event &&
       (event.ctrlKey || event.metaKey || event.altKey) &&
       gBrowser.selectedTab.pinned
     ) {
+      let pinnedTab = gBrowser.selectedTab;
       if (gBrowser.visibleTabs.length > gBrowser.pinnedTabCount) {
         gBrowser.tabContainer.selectedIndex = gBrowser.pinnedTabCount;
       }
+      // The Home tab is exempt: it is the one pinned tab that must stay
+      // available, and it is hidden from the strip, so an unloaded Home would
+      // be an invisible tab that reloads itself the next time the Home button
+      // is pressed -- a cost with no visible benefit.
+      if (pinnedTab.hasAttribute("cthulhu-home-tab")) {
+        return;
+      }
+
+      const unloadPinnedTab = () => {
+        if (gBrowser.discardBrowser(pinnedTab)) {
+          // Upstream sets this only when forcing, to mark a tab the user
+          // unloaded on purpose rather than one restored lazily. That is
+          // precisely this case, so mark it and get the faded-tab styling.
+          pinnedTab.toggleAttribute("discarded", true);
+        }
+      };
+
+      // Do not tear the browser down while the async tab switcher is still
+      // switching away from it: clearing its layers mid-switch lands in
+      // AsyncTabSwitcher.onLayersCleared() with the tab in an unexpected
+      // state, which trips its internal assertion -- a dump() in release
+      // builds, but a throw in DEBUG ones. gBrowser._switcher exists only for
+      // the duration of a switch and is torn down immediately before
+      // TabSwitchDone fires, so it doubles as "is a switch in flight".
+      if (gBrowser._switcher) {
+        gBrowser.addEventListener("TabSwitchDone", unloadPinnedTab, {
+          once: true,
+        });
+      } else {
+        unloadPinnedTab();
+      }
       return;
     }
 
